Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 20:59:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 024/154] atrium: assert the favicon cache bound instead of
 reading it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The 256-entry cap and its eviction were only ever verified by inspection. That
is the weakest claim in the task, and the bound is the entire reason the type
exists: tab ids are minted forever, so an unbounded map here grows for the life
of the process.

BoundedIconStore extracts the storage, dependency-free - no content::, no
threading, just std - so it is a fast standalone gtest unit. Same posture as
slot_presentation_table and edit_focus_gate, shared by //chrome/browser/ui:ui
and :unit_tests through a diamond rather than compiled twice.

Eight cases. Three are load-bearing beyond "it stores things":

  - a thousand stores into a three-entry cache never exceed capacity, asserted
    on every iteration rather than only at the end;
  - a re-store does NOT renew an entry's place in the queue, so one busy tab
    re-storing on every favicon change cannot hold every other entry alive -
    that is the failure the bound exists to prevent, and insertion-order
    eviction is deliberate rather than an incomplete LRU;
  - empty bytes are ignored, so a tab with no icon cannot evict one that has
    an icon.

FaviconCache keeps its thread assertion and delegates the rest. Behaviour is
unchanged: make favicon-probe still 4/4 and make swipe-probe 15/15 after the
extraction.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
